# Cold Start step 4.7. One of the two things on that page that cannot be applied
# afterwards, which is why it ships in the template rather than being a task.
#
# WHAT IT PREVENTS. A carriage return at the end of a script's first line makes the
# kernel look for an interpreter called "sh\r", which does not exist. The error is
# "bad interpreter: no such file or directory" and it names the interpreter, so it
# reads as a missing shell rather than as a broken file. Nothing you can see in the
# file is wrong. It costs an afternoon the first time, and it happens the moment a
# script written on Windows reaches a Linux machine or a cluster.
#
# Retroactively this is a pain: files already committed with CRLF keep it, and
# fixing them is a renormalise commit that touches everything and hides real changes
# in the diff. Set on day one it costs nothing at all.

# Let git decide what is text, then store it with LF in the repository.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The ones where it is not a preference.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
*.R    text eol=lf

# Windows tooling that genuinely wants CRLF.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf

# Never touched, because "text=auto" guessing wrong on a binary corrupts it.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.mat  binary
*.npy  binary
*.h5   binary
*.nc   binary
